issue_context.body:
The `Module` type has an `options` property whose type is `NuxtOptions`:

https://github.com/nuxt/nuxt.js/blob/f08f4efcde7bc174fd9b68a2a5a785d504659391/packages/types/config/module.d.ts#L24

Since it's `NuxtOptions` and not `NuxtConfig`, if a module extends `NuxtConfig` with an extra property, that won't be accessible type-wise from the module. For example:

```ts
declare module '@nuxt/types' {
  interface NuxtConfig {
    i18n?: Options
  }
}
```

```js
/** @type {import('@nuxt/types').Module} */
export default function () {
  const moduleOptions = this.options.i18n  // `this.options.i18n` has type `any` here
}
```

### Versions

- nuxt: v2.15.2

comments:
> Those are the same objects anyway, as you said, but this.options have proper types so it's clearer to reason about.

The reason is that nuxt typing are incomplete. `Nuxt` is not typed at all.

> I agree that it should be NuxtOptions because the Nuxt options themselves are resolved but then the module must extend NuxtOptions or else its types won't be available to itself

The module should internally resolve options from input key(s) and inline options. It is not reliable outside the context of the module itself. Here is a proper usage:

```ts
interface I18nOptions {
 example: string
}

declare module '@nuxt/types' {
  interface NuxtConfig {
    i18n?: Partial<I18nOptions>
  }
}

export default function (inlineOptions) {
  const moduleOptions: I18nOptions = defu(inlineOptions, this.options.i18n, {
    example: 'default'
  })

  // moduleOptions is properly typed and normalized
}
```

Or using jsdocs:

```js
/** @type {import('@nuxt/types').Module} */
export default function (inlineOptions) {
  /** @type {import('../types').I18nOptions } */
  const moduleOptions = defu(inlineOptions, this.options.i18n, {
    example: 'default'
  })
}
```

BTW plan for the future is to simplify the process above for combining normalization, defaults, type generation and automatically register types for modules with `defineNuxtModule`. Still `NuxtConfig` gets extended (which is types for `nuxt.config` with optional and unresolved keys)
